  "query_preview": "..\n\nA dry airmass remains in place this afternoon, as indicated by \nsurface dew pts mainly in the upper 40s to to mid-50s. Scattered-\nbroken flat cu field over the southern half of the CWA should \nquickly fade early this evening. Sprawling high pressure over the \nwestern Great Lakes will slowly slide through the Central Great \nLakes tonight and tomorrow as an upper-level ridge axis moves \noverhead Thursday night. Modest return flow late in the day will \nallow temperatures and dew points to be 2-5 degrees higher compared \nthan today.\n\nThere is a bit more clarity for Friday and the weekend, as model \ntrends and ensemble members have converged toward deeper and faster \namplification. Thus, a deepening 500 MB trough/low developing over \nIowa on Friday dives southeast Friday night. Even so, there is a \nseparate 850-700 MB Theta-E ridge in place over lower Michigan on \nFriday with enough mid/upper level troughing/height falls arriving \nFriday evening/night to support showers. NBM PoPs have subsequently \nincreased into the likely category for Friday night and Saturday. \nTotal QPF remains uncertain, as the amount of weak instability still \nneeds to be sorted out. Euro ensembles suggest the probability of \n0.5 inch or more in a 24 hour period is still under 50 percent. The \nsecond half of the weekend looks drier now, based on the northern \nstream trough and next round of height falls mainly tracking through \nLake Huron, with main push of cold advection directed into the \neastern Great Lakes. None-the-less, with a weak frontal \nboundary/inverted surface trough lingering through the day over \nsoutheast Michigan, slight chance PoPs for showers/thunderstorms \nseems warranted, though instability looks to be low with weak mid \nlevel lapse rates indicated.\n\nA pronounced upper-level ridge returns for early next week, \nsupporting dry weather and warming temperatures that could \npotentially reach 90 degrees by mid week, depending on timing of a \ncold front as the upper-level flow over the northern CONUS trends \npseudo-zonal.",
